\title{The Dihedral Dial of $x^5+20x+32$ Carries Exactly Half a Bit of Hint}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{October 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We study how much information two residue views of a semiprime $N=pq$ carry about the splitting types of a fixed polynomial modulo the hidden factors $p$ and $q$. The \emph{joint view} records $(p+q,\,q-p)\bmod m$, and the \emph{product view} records $N\bmod m$. The difference of the two mutual informations is the \emph{hint value}. For the solvable quintic $f=x^5+20x+32$, whose Galois group is the dihedral group $D_5$ of order $10$, a battery of semiprimes read at the modulus $m^*=320$ gave the empirical hint value $+0.6940$ bits. We explain and evaluate this number exactly. First, we identify the residue \emph{dial} of $f$ as the Kronecker character $\chi_{20}=\leg{-5}{\cdot}$ of $\Q(\sqrt{-5})$. The identity with the Legendre symbol follows from quadratic reciprocity, and its agreement with the root counts of $f$ is checked for every prime $7\le p<100$. Second, in the Chebotarev model the hint value is exactly $\tfrac12$ bit for unordered labels and exactly $1$ bit for ordered labels. Third, a general \emph{pinned-dial law} $I(T;D)=H(D)$, valid whenever the dial is a function of the label, explains the structure of these values. Fourth, the same constants hold for the $D_3$ field of $x^3-2$, even though its label entropy is different, so the hint value is a dihedral invariant. Finally, the joint view at $m=320$ determines both characters, while the product view determines only their product, and we exhibit two explicit prime pairs that the product view merges. The reported $0.6940$ lies strictly between the unordered limit $\tfrac12$ and the ordered limit $1$. Plug-in estimates on growing batteries decrease toward $\tfrac12$.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

Let $f\in\Z[x]$ be a fixed polynomial. For a prime $p$, the factorization pattern of $f\bmod p$ (and in particular the number of roots of $f$ in $\F_p$) is governed by the Frobenius conjugacy class of $p$ in the Galois group $G$ of the splitting field of $f$. For a semiprime $N=pq$ the pair of patterns at $p$ and $q$ is hidden information about the factors. We ask how much of it is visible through cheap residue views at a modulus $m$:
\begin{itemize}
\item the \emph{product view} $V_{\mathrm{prod}}=N\bmod m$, which is computable from $N$ alone;
\item the \emph{joint view} $V_{\mathrm{joint}}=\big((p+q)\bmod m,\ (q-p)\bmod m\big)$, which requires the factors.
\end{itemize}
If $T$ denotes the label (the pair of splitting types), the \emph{hint value} is
\[
\Hint = I(T;V_{\mathrm{joint}})-I(T;V_{\mathrm{prod}}).
\]
Our running example is $f(x)=x^5+20x+32$, with Galois group $D_5$. At $m^*=320$ the measured value is $\Hint\approx+0.6940$ bits. We identify the dial (Section~\ref{sec:dial}), prove the exact Chebotarev-limit hint law (Section~\ref{sec:law}), isolate the pinned-dial law (Section~\ref{sec:pinned}), establish dihedral universality for $D_3$ and $D_5$ (Section~\ref{sec:d3}), analyse the residue views at $320$ (Section~\ref{sec:views}), and reconcile the measured value with the limit (Section~\ref{sec:verdict}).

\paragraph{Notation.} Logarithms are to base $2$. For a finite set $\Omega$ with the uniform distribution and functions $T,V$ on $\Omega$,
\[
H(T)=-\sum_t \Pr[T=t]\log\Pr[T=t],\quad H(T\mid V)=\sum_v\Pr[V=v]\,H(T\mid V=v),\quad I(T;V)=H(T)-H(T\mid V).
\]

\section{The dial of $x^5+20x+32$}\label{sec:dial}

\begin{proposition}[Square discriminant]
The discriminant of $x^5+ax+b$ is $5^5b^4+4^4a^5$. For $(a,b)=(20,32)$ it equals $64000^2$, where $64000=2^9\cdot5^3$. Hence the Galois group of $f$ lies in $A_5$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proposition}[One real root]
The map $x\mapsto x^5+20x+32$ is strictly increasing on $\mathbb{R}$, and $f$ has exactly one real root, which lies in $(-2,-1)$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
If $x<y$ then $x^5<y^5$, so $f(x)<f(y)$. Since $f(-2)=-40<0<11=f(-1)$, the intermediate value theorem gives a root, and monotonicity gives uniqueness.
\end{proof}

Consequently complex conjugation fixes exactly one root and swaps the other four in two pairs. In $D_5$ such an element is a \emph{reflection}, so the unique quadratic subfield of the splitting field is imaginary.

\begin{definition}
Let $\chi_{20}(n)=c(n\bmod 20)$, where $c(r)=+1$ for $r\in\{1,3,7,9\}$, $c(r)=-1$ for $r\in\{11,13,17,19\}$, and $c(r)=0$ otherwise.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Complete multiplicativity]
$\chi_{20}(ab)=\chi_{20}(a)\chi_{20}(b)$ for all $a,b\in\mathbb{N}$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Both sides depend only on $a,b\bmod 20$; check the $400$ residue pairs.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The dial is $\Q(\sqrt{-5})$]\label{thm:dial}
For every prime $p\notin\{2,5\}$, $\leg{-5}{p}=\chi_{20}(p)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
We have $\leg{-5}{p}=\leg{-1}{p}\leg{5}{p}$. The first supplement gives $\leg{-1}{p}=+1$ iff $p\equiv1\pmod 4$. Since $5\equiv1\pmod4$, reciprocity gives $\leg5p=\leg p5$, which is $+1$ iff $p\equiv\pm1\pmod5$. The product depends only on $p\bmod20$ and reproduces the table of $c$ on the eight admissible residues.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Root counts follow the dial]\label{thm:roots}
For each of the $22$ primes $7\le p<100$, $f$ has $0$, $1$ or $5$ roots modulo $p$, and it has exactly one root if and only if $\chi_{20}(p)=-1$, equivalently $\leg{-5}{p}=-1$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Direct enumeration of $f(x)\bmod p$ for $x\in\{0,\dots,p-1\}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
For the $427$ primes $7\le p<3000$, $f$ has $0$ roots $174$ times, $1$ root $221$ times and $5$ roots $32$ times, and never any other number. The equivalence of Theorem~\ref{thm:roots} holds for all $427$ primes. Among $\Q(\sqrt d)$ with $d\in\{-1,\pm2,\pm5,\pm10\}$, only $d=-5$ matches. The conductor $20$ explains why the modulus must be divisible by $20$ (and by $40$ for the joint view, see Section~\ref{sec:views}), as $m^*=320=2^4\cdot20$ is.
\end{remark}

\section{The dihedral Chebotarev box}

We realize $D_n$ as the $2n$ maps $\sigma_{e,b}:y\mapsto(-1)^ey+b$ of $\Z/n$, with $e\in\{0,1\}$ and $b\in\Z/n$, encoded by $x=ne+b$. Rotations have $e=0$ and reflections have $e=1$.

\begin{definition}
For $x\in D_n$, the \emph{label} $T(x)$ is the number of fixed points of $\sigma_x$, which corresponds to the number of roots modulo $p$. The \emph{dial} is $D(x)=e$, which is $0$ for $\chi=+1$ and $1$ for $\chi=-1$.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[Labels are cycle types, $n=5$]\label{prop:cycle}
On $D_5$: $T=5$ for the identity, $T=0$ for nontrivial rotations and $T=1$ for reflections. Reflections are involutions and rotations satisfy $\sigma^5=\mathrm{id}$. The labels correspond to the factorization types $[1^5]$, $[5]$ and $[1,2,2]$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
The map $y\mapsto -y+b$ fixes $y$ iff $2y=b$, which has a unique solution mod $5$, and $-(-y+b)+b=y$. The map $y\mapsto y+b$ has fixed points iff $b=0$, and its $k$-th iterate is $y\mapsto y+kb$.
\end{proof}

For every odd $n$ the reflections are exactly the elements with one fixed point, and the rotations have $0$ or $n$.

\begin{definition}[Chebotarev model]
The semiprime box is $D_n\times D_n$ with the uniform distribution (independent, uniform Frobenius elements). For $(x_1,x_2)$ set $\Tord=(T(x_1),T(x_2))$, $\Tun=\{T(x_1),T(x_2)\}$, $\Dpair=(D(x_1),D(x_2))$ (the joint view read through the dial) and $\Dprod=D(x_1)+D(x_2)\bmod2$ (the product view read through the dial, $\chi(N)$). The Chebotarev-limit hint value is
\[
\Hint_n(T)=I(T;\Dpair)-I(T;\Dprod).
\]
\end{definition}

This is a model of the large-battery limit. It assumes Chebotarev equidistribution and that the residue views see the label only through $\chi_{20}$ (Section~\ref{sec:views}).

\section{The $D_5$ hint law}\label{sec:law}

\begin{theorem}[The prime-level dial is pinned]
On $D_5$: $H(T)=\tfrac15+\tfrac12\log5$, $H(T\mid D)=\tfrac12\log5-\tfrac45$, and $I(T;D)=1=H(D)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The label distribution is $(\tfrac1{10},\tfrac4{10},\tfrac5{10})$. Given a reflection the label is constant. Given a rotation it is $(\tfrac15,\tfrac45)$, with entropy $\log5-\tfrac85$. Averaging and subtracting gives the result.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[$D_5$ hint law, unordered labels]\label{thm:d5un}
On $D_5\times D_5$:
\[
H(\Tun)=\log5-\tfrac9{50},\qquad H(\Tun\mid\Dpair)=\log5-\tfrac{42}{25},\qquad H(\Tun\mid\Dprod)=\log5-\tfrac{59}{50}.
\]
Hence $I(\Tun;\Dpair)=\tfrac32$, $I(\Tun;\Dprod)=1$, and
\[
\boxed{\ \Hint_5(\Tun)=\tfrac12\ }.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The unordered labels $\{5,5\},\{0,5\},\{0,0\},\{1,5\},\{0,1\},\{1,1\}$ occur $1,8,16,10,40,25$ times among the $100$ cells. The four pair-dial fibres ($25$ cells each) have label counts $(1,8,16)$, $(5,20)$, $(5,20)$ and $(25)$. The two product-dial fibres ($50$ cells each) have counts $(1,8,16,25)$ and $(10,40)$. Averaging fibre entropies and subtracting gives the result.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Ordered labels]\label{thm:d5ord}
On $D_5\times D_5$: $H(\Tord)=\tfrac25+\log5$, $H(\Tord\mid\Dpair)=\log5-\tfrac85$ and $H(\Tord\mid\Dprod)=\log5-\tfrac35$. Hence $I(\Tord;\Dpair)=2$, $I(\Tord;\Dprod)=1$ and $\Hint_5(\Tord)=1$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The ordered distribution is a product of two copies of the prime-level one. The product-dial fibres have counts $(1,4,4,16,25)$ and $(5,20,5,20)$, each with entropy $\log5-\tfrac35$.
\end{proof}

\section{The pinned-dial law}\label{sec:pinned}

\begin{theorem}[Pinned-dial law]\label{thm:pinned}
Let $\Omega$ be finite and nonempty with the uniform distribution, $T:\Omega\to\mathcal B$ and $D:\Omega\to\mathcal C$. If $D=g\circ T$ on $\Omega$ for some $g:\mathcal B\to\mathcal C$, then $I(T;D)=H(D)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Since $D$ is determined by $T$, $H(T,D)=H(T)$. Then $I(T;D)=H(T)-H(T\mid D)=H(T)-(H(T,D)-H(D))=H(D)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[The product row is pinned]
On $D_5\times D_5$, $\Dprod=1$ iff exactly one entry of $\Tun$ equals $1$. Hence $I(\Tun;\Dprod)=H(\Dprod)=1$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
By Proposition~\ref{prop:cycle}, label $1$ means reflection. The product dial is balanced ($50$ cells each). Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:pinned}.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[The pair row is not pinned]
$I(\Tun;\Dpair)=\tfrac32<2=H(\Dpair)$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}[Structural proof]
$\Tun$ determines the unordered pair of dials. When the dials agree the ordered pair is determined. When they differ, which happens with probability $\tfrac12$, the two orders are equally likely by symmetry. So $H(\Dpair\mid\Tun)=\tfrac12$ and $I=2-\tfrac12=\tfrac32$. This is the entropy of an unordered pair of fair bits, with distribution $(\tfrac14,\tfrac12,\tfrac14)$.
\end{proof}

Thus $\Hint_5(\Tun)=H(\{\chi(p),\chi(q)\})-H(\chi(p)\chi(q))=\tfrac32-1=\tfrac12$. With ordered labels both rows are pinned and the value is $2-1=1$.

\section{Dihedral universality: the $D_3$ control}\label{sec:d3}

The cubic $x^3-2$ has Galois group $D_3\cong S_3$ and quadratic subfield $\Q(\sqrt{-3})$.

\begin{theorem}[$D_3$ hint law]
On $D_3\times D_3$:
\begin{align*}
H(\Tun)&=\log3+\tfrac{13}{18}, & H(\Tun\mid\Dpair)&=\log3-\tfrac79, & H(\Tun\mid\Dprod)&=\log3-\tfrac5{18},\\
H(\Tord)&=\log3+\tfrac43, & H(\Tord\mid\Dpair)&=\log3-\tfrac23, & H(\Tord\mid\Dprod)&=\log3+\tfrac13.
\end{align*}
Hence $\Hint_3(\Tun)=\tfrac12$ and $\Hint_3(\Tord)=1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Dihedral universality for $n=3,5$]
$\Hint_3(\Tun)=\Hint_5(\Tun)=\tfrac12$ and $\Hint_3(\Tord)=\Hint_5(\Tord)=1$, while $H_{D_5}(\Tun)=\log5-\tfrac9{50}<\log3+\tfrac{13}{18}=H_{D_3}(\Tun)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The equalities follow from the preceding theorems. For the inequality, $\log3>1.58$ and $\log5<2.33$.
\end{proof}

Exact computation in the same model gives $\tfrac12$ and $1$ for $n=7,9,11$. For even $n$ the law fails ($n=4$ gives about $0.319$ bits unordered), because reflections then have $0$ or $2$ fixed points.

\section{The residue views at $m^*=320$}\label{sec:views}

\begin{theorem}[The joint view pins both characters]
If $p+q\equiv p'+q'$ and $q-p\equiv q'-p'\pmod{320}$, then $p\equiv p'$ and $q\equiv q'\pmod{160}$. In particular $\chi_{20}(p)=\chi_{20}(p')$ and $\chi_{20}(q)=\chi_{20}(q')$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Adding and subtracting gives $2q\equiv2q'$ and $2p\equiv2p'\pmod{320}$. Then use $20\mid160$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}[Which moduli work]
The joint view at modulus $m$ pins the factors only modulo $m/2$, so it sees $\chi_{20}$ of each factor exactly when $40\mid m$. The product view needs only $20\mid m$. Experimentally ($B=1500$) the plug-in hint is $0.74$ at $m=320$ and $0.60$ at $m=160$, but only $0.07$ at $m=100$ and $0.11$ at $m=64$, where what remains is estimator bias.
\end{remark}

\begin{theorem}[The product view pins only the product]
If $pq\equiv p'q'\pmod{320}$ then $\chi_{20}(p)\chi_{20}(q)=\chi_{20}(p')\chi_{20}(q')$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Reduce modulo $20$ and use complete multiplicativity.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The product view merges distinct dial pairs]
The numbers $7,11,13,569$ are prime and $11\cdot13=143\equiv7\cdot569=3983\pmod{320}$. The polynomial $f$ has one root modulo $11$ and modulo $13$ ($\chi_{20}=-1$) and no root modulo $7$ and modulo $569$ ($\chi_{20}=+1$). The factor sums $24$ and $576$ differ modulo $320$, so the joint view separates the two semiprimes.
\end{theorem}

\section{Verdict and finite-sample reading}\label{sec:verdict}

\begin{theorem}[The $D_5$ dial carries a hint]
$0<\Hint_5(\Tun)=\tfrac12<0.6940<1=\Hint_5(\Tord)$.
\end{theorem}

\paragraph{Plug-in experiments.} Take all prime pairs $7\le p<q<B$ with uniform weight, unordered root-count labels, the joint view modulo $320$ and the product view modulo $320$:
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{rrccc}
\hline
$B$ & pairs & $\widehat I(T;V_{\mathrm{joint}})$ & $\widehat I(T;V_{\mathrm{prod}})$ & $\widehat\Hint$\\
\hline
500 & 4{,}186 & 1.8785 & 1.0228 & 0.8557\\
1{,}500 & 27{,}730 & 1.7398 & 1.0035 & 0.7363\\
4{,}000 & 149{,}331 & 1.6142 & 1.0006 & 0.6135\\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
With the dial-pair view $(\chi_{20}(p),\chi_{20}(q))$ the plug-in values are $1.4873,1.4979,1.4987\to\tfrac32$. The product view ($320$ cells) is at its limit. The joint view (up to $320^2$ cells) carries a decaying upward plug-in bias. The decreasing readings bracket $0.6940$ and approach $\tfrac12$. These are empirical observations, not part of the proof.

\section{Algorithms}

\begin{algorithm}[Dial identification]
For each prime $p<P$ not dividing the discriminant, count the roots $r(p)$ of $f$ in $\F_p$ by evaluation. For each candidate discriminant $d$ supported on the ramified primes, compute $\leg dp$ by Euler's criterion. Return the $d$ with $[r(p)=1]\Leftrightarrow\leg dp=-1$ on all tested $p$. The cost is $O(P^2/\log P)$. For $f$ and $P=3000$ the output is $d=-5$.
\end{algorithm}

\begin{algorithm}[Exact Chebotarev hint value]
Enumerate $D_n$, compute fixed-point labels and dials, tabulate the joint counts over the $4n^2$ cells of $D_n\times D_n$, and return $I(T;\Dpair)-I(T;\Dprod)$ with $I=H(T)+H(V)-H(T,V)$. The cost is $O(n^3)$.
\end{algorithm}

\begin{algorithm}[Plug-in battery estimator]
Sieve the primes below $B$, compute root counts, enumerate the pairs $p<q$, and return the difference of empirical mutual informations of the label with the joint and product views. The cost is $O(\pi(B)^2)$.
\end{algorithm}

\section{Discussion and future work}

A residue view of a semiprime sees only abelian information about the factors. For a dihedral group of odd degree that information is the quadratic character of the unique quadratic subfield, here $\chi_{20}$. The rotation ambiguity $H(T\mid D)=\tfrac12\log5-\tfrac45$ is invisible to every residue class. The Chebotarev model is an assumption about the limit. The identification of the dial rests on reciprocity together with a finite check of root counts.

\begin{conjecture}[Dihedral universality]
For every odd $n\ge3$, in the Chebotarev model of a $D_n$ field, the hint value of the quadratic dial is exactly $\tfrac12$ bit (unordered labels) and $1$ bit (ordered labels).
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[Plug-in bias law]
For $M$ semiprimes read through the joint view at modulus $m$, the excess of the plug-in hint value over its limit is $\frac{(K-1)(|T|-1)}{2M\ln2}+o(1/M)$, where $K$ is the number of occupied joint cells.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[Non-dihedral solvable quintics]
For the Frobenius group $F_{20}$ (e.g.\ $x^5-2$), numerical evidence suggests an unordered hint value of $\tfrac98$ and an ordered value of $\tfrac74$. For a cyclic quintic the hint value equals $H(\text{pair dial})-H(\text{product dial})$. The values for $C_5$, $D_5$ and $F_{20}$ appear to be pairwise distinct.
\end{conjecture}

\section{Conclusion}

The dial of $x^5+20x+32$ is the character $\leg{-5}{\cdot}$ of conductor $20$. Through a window of $320$ residues, the factor sum and difference reveal this character for each factor separately, while the product reveals only its product. In the Chebotarev limit this asymmetry is worth exactly half a bit about the unordered splitting types, and one bit about the ordered types. The value is the same for $D_3$ as for $D_5$, and it follows from the pinned-dial law $I(T;D)=H(D)$. The empirical $0.6940$ lies strictly between the two exact limits, and plug-in estimates on growing batteries decrease toward $\tfrac12$.

